</p/><p><strong>Summary: </strong>
Continuing a lifelong fondness of King Arthur’s court.
</p>
        <blockquote class="quote epigraph" data-id="20221009T120429">
<p>
For the world is only a tissue of masks and veils; And when the veils are
lifted, in birth or death or the other ecstasies, we look beyond them, and see
faces that we already know, but cannot bear to look at for long.
</p>
<footer>&#8213;Simon Heywood, <cite>The Legend of Vortigern</cite></footer></blockquote>
<p>Sparked, in part, by pulling out and playing <cite><a href="https://www.drivethrurpg.com/en/product/514996/mythic-bastionland?affiliate_id=318171">Mythic Bastionland</a></cite> <small><a class="ref" rel="tag opener" aria-label="Other site-wide references of “Mythic Bastionland”" title="Other site-wide references of “Mythic Bastionland”" href="https://takeonrules.com/site-map/glossary/#abbr-dfn-GLOSSARY-MYTHIC-BASTIONLAND">&#128214;</a></small>
, I have again
felt the warmth of embers rekindled; I once more delve into my fondness of
Arthuriana.</p>
<p>Part of that fondness is questing for books (and <span><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Role-playing_game">Role Playing Games</a></span> (<abbr title="Role Playing Games">RPGs</abbr> <small><a class="ref" rel="tag opener" aria-label="Other site-wide references of “Role Playing Games”" title="Other site-wide references of “Role Playing Games”" href="https://takeonrules.com/site-map/glossary/#abbr-dfn-GLOSSARY-RPG">&#128214;</a></small>)
) that would fill me with
wonder.  One past attempt was <cite data-id="37F1A94E-E29E-4745-8C05-FD507311E2C9">The Mists of Avalon</cite> by Marion Zimmer Bradley,
another <cite data-id="isbn-9780316585118">The Faerie Morgana</cite> by Louisa Morgan.  Both focusing on Morgan le Fey,
providing an interesting perspective that humanizes a woman often cast as
villain.</p>
<p>I had read, and adored, <cite data-id="A6B3DC34-C462-46BC-8CD7-D89BDC31D6EB">The Once and Future King</cite> by T.H. White, which gave
full and fair treatment of Guineverre; whereas <cite data-id="37F1A94E-E29E-4745-8C05-FD507311E2C9">The Mists of Avalon</cite> was one of
woman tears down another woman.  And <cite data-id="isbn-9780316585118">The Faerie Morgana</cite> is passable, but
there’s little economy of language.  I stumble on an observed pattern of several
different paragraphs that are comprised of several sentences that are in essence
reiteration of emotions, which lands heavy as mailed gauntlet.</p>
<p>Through serendipity, I recently found <cite data-id="isbn-9781951872700">Galahad and the Grail</cite> by Malcolm Guite,
a lyrical ballad.  I’ve been etching heraldric shields on pink erasures and
stamping them in my campaign journal; seeing the woodcut illustrations
throughout the book (and as the author’s biographical photo), I felt
providence’s pull.</p>
<p>One in which I’ve considered revisiting <cite data-id="39A47196-13AE-44F2-B7DC-601871B6072B">The Fall of Arthur</cite> by J.R.R. Tolkien,
to again hear and feel the poetry of a myth.  To find and feel the Truth in this
fictive story.</p>
<p><time datetime="2026-08-20" title="2026-08-20">Last night</time> I read the introduction of <cite data-id="isbn-9781951872700">Galahad and the Grail</cite> and some of the
appendices.  I saw that this could be a very Christ-centered retelling.  I might
normally cringe at such things, but withheld judgment.  As I read further, the
author is concerned with symbolism, mysticism, and stewardship of the land.
That I can appreciate and embrace.  It follows in my well-trod mental pathways
of Tolkien.</p>
<p>I think about my present reading of <cite data-id="isbn-9780316585118">The Faerie Morgana</cite> and weigh it against my
recent read of <cite data-id="isbn-9781250363688">The River Has Roots</cite> by Amal El-Mohtar.  When reading <cite data-id="isbn-9780316585118">The Faerie Morgana</cite> I find myself setting the book down somewhat tired of the story
and composition.  While reading <cite data-id="isbn-9781250363688">The River Has Roots</cite>, I found myself setting
down the book with a sense of fullness.</p>
<p>I don’t think this setting down of books is related to my attention span; I
spent several consecutive hours reading <cite data-id="isbn-9780593820254">Dungeon Crawler Carl</cite> by Matt Dinniman.<small class="side-container">
  <span class="side-label"><span class="hidden">(</span>Sidenote<span class="hidden">:</span></span>
  <span class="side" role="note"> A book (and series) that I marvel at being far better than it should be.
Someone recently posited to me that this series may well be a/the Don Quixote of
our era.<span class="hidden">)</span></span>
</small>
</p>
<p>The interplay of grammar, word-play, transubstantiation, conjugation, lyricism,
and tonal register of <cite data-id="isbn-9781250363688">The River Has Roots</cite> invokes a sense of splendor, magic,
eternal cycles, and loss.  I felt the slowness of soft flowing river reshaping
my brain.  Fighting against the digital attention capturing colonization I’ve
allowed to seep and squirm into my brain.</p>
<p>These are not tales of sustained progress, that manifest propagandized myth we
receive and amply, but instead of the natural rise and fall, of waves coming to
shore and receding.  That once and future, present here and now in its telling.</p>
<p>I also think of the queerness of other Arthurian stories I read: <cite data-id="B18FF3E3-E506-4883-9F81-C4BB74B32E80">Spear</cite> by Nicola Griffith and <cite data-id="C63CD739-9932-4CE4-98AE-2361E8A9A8F5">Lancelot and the Lord of the Distant Isles</cite> by Samuel N. Rosenberg and Patricia Terry.  Both holding models
of tender (non-toxic) masculinity, something faded and beleaguered from
patriarchy’s constant assault.</p>
<p>Arthuriana tells of the questing beast but is itself a questing beast.</p>

